Q5. Why did you use Spring Batch?
Answer

Spring Batch is designed for large-volume processing.

Benefits:

Chunk Processing
Job Restart Capability
Fault Tolerance
Parallel Processing
Job Monitoring


Example:

100,000 Records

Chunk Size = 500

Commit every 500 records


This reduces memory consumption.

Q6. Why store data in S3 first?
Answer

S3 acts as a landing zone.

Benefits:

Highly Durable
Cheap Storage
Large File Support
Audit History
Reprocessing Capability


If downstream systems fail:

Data remains available in S3.

No Data Loss

Q7. Why didn't you directly send data from Extraction Service to Kafka?
Answer

This is a common architect-level question.

Bad Design
External API
     |
Extraction Service
     |
Kafka


Problems:

Tight Coupling
Hard Scaling
No Reprocessing
Difficult Recovery

Better Design
Extraction Service
     |
S3
     |
Publisher Service
     |
Kafka


Benefits:

Independent Scaling
Failure Recovery
Reusable Data
Loose Coupling

Q8. What does Publisher Service do?
Answer

Responsibilities:

Read Files from S3
Parse JSON
Apply Business Rules
Transform Data
Publish Kafka Events


Example Event:

{
  "restaurantId":"123",
  "city":"New York",
  "action":"CREATE"
}

Q9. Why Kafka?
Answer

Kafka was used because multiple downstream systems consumed the same business event.

Benefits:

Asynchronous Communication
High Throughput
Scalability
Fault Tolerance
Replay Support

Q10. Why Kafka instead of REST communication?
Answer
REST
Synchronous
Tightly Coupled
Consumer Failure Affects Producer

Kafka
Asynchronous
Loose Coupling
Retry Support
Event Retention
Replay Capability


If consumer is down:

Event remains in Kafka.

No Data Loss.

